        means: b"(0.26.2) A usize local compared with 0 as if it could go below: `n >= 0` is always true \
            and `n < 0` always false, since a usize is never negative. Most often a count down from \
            a length (`let mut i = v.len() - 1; while i >= 0 { ... i -= 1; }`): a length is a usize, \
            so it never stops (the subtraction overflows: a panic, or a wrap and then the index \
            panics). Not in std.",
        fix: b"Count down in the unsigned type, testing before the step: `let mut i = v.len(); while i \
            > 0 { i -= 1; ... }` (or `for i in (0..v.len()).rev()`); or convert the length to i64 \
            where it is made (`v.len() as i64`) when the value can be negative.",
        example: b"fn main() {\n\
